%I A399912 #16 Nov 22 2026 21:39:49
%S A399912 3,9,22,27,42,78,81,111,205,242,243,272,294,342,404,465,506,584,689,
%T A399912 729,812,903,1014,1027,1096,1355,1673,2058,2162,2187,2211,2485,2662,
%U A399912 3403,3422,3502,3660,3916,4107,4624,5334,5671,6330,6394,6498,6561,7230,7439,7868
%N A399912 Numbers k such that the k-th cyclotomic polynomial evaluated at 10 (=A019328(k)) is not coprime to k.
%C A399912 Numbers k such that the Zsigmondy number Zs(k,10,1) differs from the n-th cyclotomic polynomial evaluated at 10. Note that Zs(k,10,1) = A204847(k) for k != 1, 3.
%C A399912 Numbers of the form k = p^r * ord(10,p), where p != 2, 5 is any prime, r >= 1, and ord(10,p) is the multiplicative order of 10 modulo p. For such a number k we have Zs(k,10,1) = Phi_k(10)/p. (See A323748 for more details).
%H A399912 Jianing Song, <a href="/A399912/b399912.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399912 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ZsigmondyTheorem.html">Zsigmondy Theorem</a>.
%H A399912 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zsigmondy&#39;s theorem">Zsigmondy's theorem</a>.
%e A399912 3 = 3*ord(10,3) => 37 = Zs(3,10,1) = Phi_3(10)/3 = 111/3.
%e A399912 9 = 3^2*ord(10,3) => 333667 = Zs(9,10,1) = Phi_9(10)/3 = 1001001/3.
%e A399912 22 = 11*ord(10,11) => 826446281 = Zs(22,10,1) = Phi_22(10)/11 = 9090909091/11.
%e A399912 27 = 3^3*ord(10,3) => 333333333666666667 = Zs(27,10,1) = Phi_27(10)/3 = 1000000001000000001/3.
%e A399912 42 = 7*ord(10,7) => 156985855573 = Zs(42,10,1) = Phi_42(10)/7 = 1098900989011.
%e A399912 78 = 13*ord(10,13) => 84530853761614539306847 = Zs(78,10,1) = Phi_78(10)/13 = 1098901098900989010989011/13.
%o A399912 (PARI) isA399912(n,b=10) = if(n>1, my(f=factor(n), w=#f~, p=f[w,1]); if(b%p==0, return(0)); f[w,2]=0; return(znorder(Mod(b,p))==factorback(f)), 0)
%Y A399912 Cf. A019328, A204847, A323748.
%Y A399912 Cf. A093106, A093107.
%K A399912 nonn,easy,new
%O A399912 1,1
